WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.120
SPEAKER 1: Let's take a look
at a Cloudstate Kotlin example

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.510
that uses the Lightbend
Cloudstate service to run

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.039
this on the cloud.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:11.730
And you can find the
source code on GitHub,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.760
jamesward,
cloudstate-sample-fraud.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.530
OK, first let's take
a look at the code.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.890
So with Cloudstate,
we use protobufs

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.470
to model the messages
that we're going to send

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:28.000
and then the RPC operations
that will be performed.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.950
So we have a transaction with
a time stamp, amount, location,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.810
and description.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.240
And then we have
a user transaction

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.540
that associates that with
a Cloudstate entity key

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.930
which allows us to do the
partitioning of the messages.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.400
And that also gives
us the ability

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.880
to scale this thing up and
down horizontally based

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.190
on what's happening
to the messages

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.180
as they're being partitioned.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.700
OK, and then we have a way to
add a user transaction and Git

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.840
transactions.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.290
Let's take a look at
the activity entity.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:06.150
And this is where the logic
for handling those messages

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.540
happens.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.800
So we've got an
EventSourcedEntity.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.550
And then you can see
there's a constructor

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.670
argument with the user ID
which is our partitioning key.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.030
So that's how we're able
to partition this data.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.070
In this class, we
have our transactions,

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.680
which is our mutable list.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.730
And so whenever we get
transaction messages coming in,

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.980
then we're going to be able
to add them to this list

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.420
so that we have our accumulated
state here in this entity.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.630
Then we have a command handler
for addTransaction which

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.890
takes that userTransaction.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.160
And then we also get a
CommandContext with there.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.670
And then we can have our
logic for detecting fraud

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.410
on a transaction.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.510
So whenever a new
transaction comes in,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.710
we're going to use the past
data and the new transaction

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.552
to calculate the velocity.

00:01:55.552 --> 00:01:57.510
And so there's obviously
lots of different ways

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.950
that we would compute whether
or not the transaction is

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.780
potentially fraudulent.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.120
For this simple example,
I'm just using velocity.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.979
And if the user's velocity
goes over a max velocity,

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.503
then we're going to say
that there's possible fraud.

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:14.920
And in this case,
we're just going

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.320
to put to that to the console.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.570
But then, once we have
detected the potential fraud,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:25.500
we are going to emit a message
which says, OK, we've done

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.920
our handling of this message.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.010
And then that event
handler will trigger.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.410
This will go on to an event log,
which is persisted in Spanner,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.290
in Google Cloud Spanner.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.360
OK, so that's the bulk
of the entity processing.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.210
I've got a little class that
just starts up the server.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.920
And then I've got
a simulator class

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.950
that is going to simulate
some transactions.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.550
So let's go over
and run this now.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.200
I already have
this up and running

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.330
on Google Cloud via the
Lightbend Cloudstate service.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.220
But let's go to start
up the simulator.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.830
And what this will do is start
adding a bunch of transactions

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.660
to this transaction log.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.840
And what we'll be able to
do once this starts emitting

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.660
those messages, we'll be able to
see the messages that are being

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.640
emitted here in the console.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.550
And then we'll go over
to Google Cloud Logging

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.640
to actually see if we
get a potential fraud.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.420
So here we go.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.010
We're now feeding a bunch of
transactions into that system.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.580
We've got multiple users.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.570
So we're doing partitioning
of the data for that entity.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.150
Now, here in the Logs
Viewer in Google Cloud,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.460
now we can see the
server messages

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.050
from my entity that's running
up on Google Cloud and Lightbend

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.220
Cloudstate.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.500
So let's go try to
jump to now and see.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.020
There we go.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:44.520
OK.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.430
So it looks like we just
got a possible fraud alert.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.920
And we can see the
user ID, the merchant,

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.650
the amount, when it
happened, and then

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.150
the Google Map linked to
where that transaction was.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.190
And if we let this
continue running,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.790
we probably will see some more
potential fraud alerts coming

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.488
in as that simulator runs.

00:04:05.488 --> 00:04:07.530
So you can see we're
feeding in tons of messages.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.600
They're partitioned
based on user ID.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.930
So you get the ability
to auto-scale the service

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.950
up and down.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.290
And that's great for
something like this,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.040
where the amount of
transactions that are coming in

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.190
is going to fluctuate
throughout the day,

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.670
with probably more transactions
happening in the morning

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.490
and evening, I would
guess, and less at night.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.870
And so, great.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.870
We can scale our service
automatically based on that.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.070
There is a container image
that I built and deployed

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.950
which contains my entity code.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.800
OK, so that's the quick
example of Cloudstate

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.330
running on the Lightbend
Cloudstate service.

